  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Nomada imbricata, commonly known as the Imbricate Cuckoo Nomad Bee, is a small parasitic bee in the family Apidae found throughout New England. Unlike honey bees and bumble bees, cuckoo nomad bees lay their eggs in the nests of other bee species, relying on host bees to raise their young.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Overlapping abdominal pattern",
      "description": "Distinctive imbricate (overlapping) pattern of dark and light bands on the abdomen, giving the bee a scaled appearance",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small size",
      "description": "Tiny bee, typically 8-10 mm long, much smaller than honey bees and bumble bees",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Lack of pollen baskets",
      "description": "Absence of the specialized hair structures (corbiculae) on hind legs used by other bees to carry pollen",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This bee is found in open woodlands, meadows, and gardens throughout New England where its host bee species are present.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The Imbricate Cuckoo Nomad Bee is a brood parasite that lays eggs in the nests of other bee species, particularly Andrena bees, where the host bees unknowingly provision the nomad bee larvae with food.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
